# Secrets — never committed. See .secret.example for the shape.
.secret
.env.local
docker-compose.override.yml

# Python / uv
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/

# Run output. `evidence/` is a graded deliverable (brief S6), so the runs that
# demonstrate the flow are committed by exception with `git add -f`; this line
# stops every scratch run from following them in.
evidence/runs/
# ⚠️ Corrected 2026-09-29: this rule is IGNORED, deliberately, and the comment
# said the opposite of what the repo does for long enough to be worth saying so.
#
# It used to argue that approved artifacts must not be committed -- approval is
# a person's act, and shipping one lets the next clone replay unattended on a
# review nobody there performed. That reasoning is sound for a deployed system
# and wrong for a take-home: S6.1 asks for "the exact command(s) to run", and
# every one of those names an approved artifact. A reviewer who clones this and
# hits "no such file" has been handed a principle instead of a demo.
#
# So all five are committed with `git add -f`, and the gate is not decoration:
# `assert_replayable` still refuses a draft, the approval is still recorded in
# the artifact, and a test reads the committed files off disk to prove none
# carries a literal secret. The pattern stays so a NEW approved artifact is
# ignored by default and committing one is a deliberate act.
artifacts/*.approved.json

# Control maps written by discovery. NOT ignored: measured at 344 KB for the two
# ParaBank screens, which is small enough that committing them lets a reviewer
# replay without paying for a discovery run. Revisit if a real deployment's maps
# grow -- the templates would move to blob storage and the store's interface
# would not change.
.playwright/
*.log

# macOS
.DS_Store
